VALUES (
  'ibir-11',
  'INTERACTIVE_READING',
  'B2',
  '{"passageTitle":"The Mathematics of Voting Systems","passageText":"A voting system is a machine for turning preferences into a single outcome, and every machine has quirks. Under first-past-the-post, a candidate can win with a small share of the vote when the opposition is divided. Ranked-choice voting avoids that problem but can eliminate a broadly acceptable candidate early. Arrow’s theorem proves that no ranked system can satisfy a short list of reasonable fairness conditions at once. The practical lesson is not that elections are meaningless, but that the choice of rule shapes the result before any ballot is cast. Designers therefore debate thresholds, district sizes and transfer rules with the same seriousness that candidates debate policy, because the rules determine which coalitions can win.","gapSentence":{"before":"A voting system is a machine for turning preferences into a single outcome, and every machine has quirks. Under first-past-the-post, a candidate can win with a small share of the vote when the opposition is divided. Ranked-choice voting avoids that problem but can ","after":" a broadly acceptable candidate early. Arrow’s theorem proves that no ranked system can satisfy a short list of reasonable fairness conditions at once. The practical lesson is not that elections are meaningless, but that the choice of rule shapes the result before any ballot is cast. Designers therefore debate thresholds, district sizes and transfer rules with the same seriousness that candidates debate policy, because the rules determine which coalitions can win.","correct":"eliminate","options":["combine","exclude","delay","eliminate"]},"titleQuestion":{"question":"Choose the most appropriate academic title for this passage:","options":["A History of Secret Ballots","How Parliaments Draft Legislation","Campaign Finance Explained","The Mathematics of Voting Systems"],"correctIndex":3},"mainIdeaQuestion":{"question":"What is the primary idea of the passage?","options":["Electoral rules systematically shape outcomes, so their design is a substantive political choice.","All voting systems produce identical results.","Arrow’s theorem proves that voting should be abolished.","District size has no effect on representation."],"correctIndex":0},"highlightPrompt":"Highlight the sentence that carries the key claim described in the question.","sentenceOptions":["Leaves also release water vapour, cooling the air around a street by several degrees and lowering the electricity demand of nearby buildings.","During deep sleep, the hippocampus replays sequences of the day in compressed bursts, while the cortex gradually incorporates those patterns into longer-term storage.","Because the receipts were lighter than the coins, they circulated as payment, and the warehouses gradually became banks.","Arrow’s theorem proves that no ranked system can satisfy a short list of reasonable fairness conditions at once."]}',
  '{"gapCorrect":"eliminate","titleCorrectIndex":3,"mainIdeaCorrectIndex":0,"sentenceCorrectIndex":3,"highlightCorrectSubstring":"Arrow’s theorem proves that no ranked system can satisfy a short list of reasonable fairness conditions at once."}',
