  '{"passageTitle":"Why Names Are Hard to Remember","passageText":"Forgetting a name is not usually a sign that memory is failing in general. Names are arbitrary labels attached to people, and they carry little meaning that would help retrieval. A face, a voice and a context are stored in rich detail, but the name is a thin link to that bundle. Retrieval also depends on attention at the moment of learning: a name heard in a noisy room may never be encoded properly. Strategies that work include repeating the name, linking it to an image and using it soon afterwards. The experience of having a name on the tip of the tongue is a retrieval failure, not evidence that the information has vanished.","gapSentence":{"before":"Forgetting a name is not usually a sign that memory is failing in general. Names are ","after":" labels attached to people, and they carry little meaning that would help retrieval. A face, a voice and a context are stored in rich detail, but the name is a thin link to that bundle. Retrieval also depends on attention at the moment of learning: a name heard in a noisy room may never be encoded properly. Strategies that work include repeating the name, linking it to an image and using it soon afterwards. The experience of having a name on the tip of the tongue is a retrieval failure, not evidence that the information has vanished.","correct":"arbitrary","options":["lengthy","arbitrary","formal","seasonal"]},"titleQuestion":{"question":"Choose the most appropriate academic title for this passage:","options":["The History of Surnames","Why Names Are Hard to Remember","The Anatomy of the Brain","How to Improve Exam Scores"],"correctIndex":1},"mainIdeaQuestion":{"question":"What is the primary idea of the passage?","options":["Names are hard to recall because they are arbitrary labels with weak retrieval cues.","Forgetting a name proves that memory is declining.","Names are stored with more meaning than faces.","Tip-of-the-tongue states mean the memory is gone."],"correctIndex":0},"highlightPrompt":"Highlight the sentence that carries the key claim described in the question.","sentenceOptions":["Because the receipts were lighter than the coins, they circulated as payment, and the warehouses gradually became banks.","A face, a voice and a context are stored in rich detail, but the name is a thin link to that bundle.","Leaves also release water vapour, cooling the air around a street by several degrees and lowering the electricity demand of nearby buildings.","During deep sleep, the hippocampus replays sequences of the day in compressed bursts, while the cortex gradually incorporates those patterns into longer-term storage."]}',
  '{"gapCorrect":"arbitrary","titleCorrectIndex":1,"mainIdeaCorrectIndex":0,"sentenceCorrectIndex":1,"highlightCorrectSubstring":"A face, a voice and a context are stored in rich detail, but the name is a thin link to that bundle."}',
